---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';

const posts = (await getCollection('blog')).sort(
  (a, b) => b.data.date.valueOf() - a.data.date.valueOf(),
);
const fmt = (d: Date) =>
  d.toLocaleDateString('en-SG', { year: 'numeric', month: 'short', day: '2-digit' });
---

<Base
  title="Knowledge base — case studies, the failure log & the field guide — theinvalid.me"
  description="Case studies, the failure log (decisions and what they cost), and an interactive field guide to running LLMs locally — the build log of a sovereign AI stack, failures included."
>
  <h1>Knowledge base</h1>
  <p style="color: var(--muted)">
    Everything I've learned building a sovereign AI stack, in public: the proof
    (case studies), the failure log (why abandoned plans get written up — the
    dead ends were the expensive part), and the interactive field guide.
  </p>

  <article class="card" style="border-color: var(--accent)">
    <h3>📐 <a href="/calculator">The local-AI field guide</a></h3>
    <p class="oneliner">Interactive: what actually fits in your VRAM, what runs on each GPU tier, how many users one card serves, and when local beats cloud.</p>
    <p class="meta">measured on an RTX 5090 · every number editable · <a href="/calculator">open the field guide →</a></p>
  </article>

  <h2 id="case-studies">Case studies</h2>
  <p style="color:var(--muted)">
    Problem → solution → outcome. Built and measured on my own stack — where a
    number is from a real run I say so, and where something didn't work I say
    that too. Client engagements get added here (with permission) as they happen.
  </p>

  <article class="card">
    <h3>Comic page → narrated video, fully local</h3>
    <p><strong>Problem.</strong> Turning static comic/manga pages into dramatized video normally means manual editing per panel, or cloud services that ingest the artwork. A webtoon creator wants automation <em>and</em> wants the unreleased art to never leave their machine.</p>
    <p><strong>Solution.</strong> A pipeline (<a href="/projects/comic-narrator">Comic Narrator</a>) that detects panels with a vision LLM + OpenCV, voices each character with a local clone, narrates captions, and drives a Ken-Burns + 2.5D parallax camera — all on one RTX 5090, nothing cloud.</p>
    <p><strong>Outcome.</strong> Page-to-video runs end to end and is in active hardening on real, full-length series pages. Honest status: the hard problems (panel-order logic, model output variance, VRAM co-residence of a 30B LLM + TTS) are documented as they're solved — see the failure log below.</p>
    <p class="meta">local · vision LLM · voice clone · ffmpeg · zero cloud</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card">
    <h3>Removing an object from production footage</h3>
    <p><strong>Problem.</strong> A clip needs an object gone — cleanly, temporally stable, without shipping the footage to a cloud VFX tool.</p>
    <p><strong>Solution.</strong> Netflix's VOID inpainting + SAM3 video-mask tracking, run locally (<a href="/projects/creative-stack">Creative Stack</a>), with a 4K finishing pass.</p>
    <p><strong>Outcome.</strong> On representative footage: <strong>clean removal in 180 seconds, no ghost, background reconstructed perfectly</strong>. On a worst-case clip (subject behind glassware, low light) it leaves a residue — and I tell clients that up front, with a free sample frame before any quote. The <a href="/blog/object-removal-arc">full honest write-up</a> shows both.</p>
    <p class="meta">VOID · SAM3 · SeedVR2 4K · 180s/clip · measured, failures included</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card">
    <h3>A sovereign LLM that physically can't leak</h3>
    <p><strong>Problem.</strong> A team wants an AI assistant but is bound by data rules — nothing sensitive may touch OpenAI or Anthropic.</p>
    <p><strong>Solution.</strong> The <a href="/projects/sovereign-ai-stack">Sovereign Stack</a>: vLLM + Nemotron on local hardware with routing-enforced privacy tiers — the "client" profile <em>refuses to fall back to cloud at all</em>, failing loudly instead of degrading silently. Tailscale-only, default-deny firewall, monitored backups.</p>
    <p><strong>Outcome.</strong> Daily-operated on my own machine at <strong>up to 268 tokens/sec</strong> (observed under real multimodal load), 180K context. The guarantee isn't a policy promise — it's enforced by the routing, and the whole thing is public so a client can audit exactly how before trusting it.</p>
    <p class="meta">vLLM · Nemotron · routing-enforced privacy · 268 tok/s observed</p>
  </article>

  <p style="margin-top:1.5rem">
    <a href="/services">→ What this costs and how to start</a>
  </p>

  <h2 id="failure-log">The failure log</h2>
  <p style="color: var(--muted)">
    New posts as the work happens.
    <span style="font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.9rem">
      Looking for something specific? <a href="/blog/topics"><strong>Browse by topic →</strong></a>
      — every post grouped by technology (LLM serving, GPU/VRAM, creative AI, audio, ops…).
    </span>
  </p>
  <ul class="postlist">
    {posts.map((post) => (
      <li>
        <time datetime={post.data.date.toISOString()}>{fmt(post.data.date)}</time>
        <span>
          <a href={`/blog/${post.id}`}>{post.data.title}</a>
          <br /><span style="color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.9rem">{post.data.description}</span>
        </span>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>
</Base>
